    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186115393,
      "id": "d54a6dbe-42cb-5614-ae0b-611de1719b45",
      "title": "Using the Product Rules for Differentiation"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "a7e78595-0d04-5cab-8a9e-7d1efabdae77",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Find $(\\mathbf{f}\\times \\mathbf{g})^{\\prime}(t)$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t) & =⟨2 t,\\,2 t^{2},\\,2⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{g}(t) & =⟨- t^{2},\\,2,\\,3 - 2 t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Use the cross product rule, preserving the factor order:\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}\\times \\mathbf{g})^{\\prime}(t)=\\mathbf{f}^{\\prime}(t)\\times \\mathbf{g}(t)+\\mathbf{f}(t)\\times \\mathbf{g}^{\\prime}(t)\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}^{\\prime}_{1}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 t)=2 \\\\ \\mathbf{f}^{\\prime}_{2}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 t^{2})=4 t \\\\ \\mathbf{f}^{\\prime}_{3}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2)=0 \\\\ \\mathbf{f}^{\\prime}(t) & =⟨2,\\,4 t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{g}^{\\prime}_{1}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(- t^{2})=- 2 t \\\\ \\mathbf{g}^{\\prime}_{2}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2)=0 \\\\ \\mathbf{g}^{\\prime}_{3}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3 - 2 t)=-2 \\\\ \\mathbf{g}^{\\prime}(t) & =⟨- 2 t,\\,0,\\,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the first product-rule term and the second product-rule term, denoted $P_1$ and $P_2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_1 & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & 4 t & 0 \\\\ - t^{2} & 2 & 3 - 2 t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ P_{1,1} & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 t & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 - 2 t\\end{vmatrix}=- 8 t^{2} + 12 t \\\\ P_{1,2} & =-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ - t^{2} & 3 - 2 t\\end{vmatrix}=4 t - 6 \\\\ P_{1,3} & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 t \\\\ - t^{2} & 2\\end{vmatrix}=4 t^{3} + 4 \\\\ P_1 & =⟨- 8 t^{2} + 12 t,\\,4 t - 6,\\,4 t^{3} + 4⟩ \\\\ P_2 & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 t & 2 t^{2} & 2 \\\\ - 2 t & 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ P_{2,1} & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 t^{2} & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}=- 4 t^{2} \\\\ P_{2,2} & =-\\begin{vmatrix}2 t & 2 \\\\ - 2 t & -2\\end{vmatrix}=0 \\\\ P_{2,3} & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 t & 2 t^{2} \\\\ - 2 t & 0\\end{vmatrix}=4 t^{3} \\\\ P_2 & =⟨- 4 t^{2},\\,0,\\,4 t^{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the two terms componentwise:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}\\times \\mathbf{g})^{\\prime}(t) & =P_1+P_2 \\\\  & =⟨- 12 t^{2} + 12 t,\\,4 t - 6,\\,8 t^{3} + 4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
